{
 "result": "sortints.py reads whitespace-separated integers from stdin and prints them sorted one per line; on my fixed-seed test file (gen.py, seed 12345, 1,000,000 ints in \u00b110^9, 10.4 MB) it runs in about 0.25\u20130.35 s end to end including interpreter startup, with timings varying that much between runs on this machine. The fast path uses numpy: bytes.translate classifies characters, token boundaries come from flatnonzero, digits are parsed with SWAR arithmetic on 8-byte windows (up to 16 digits), a packed value-plus-index key is sorted, and output is built by copying each original token with a 16-byte overlapping write. Anything the fast path does not handle (inputs under 4096 bytes, '+' signs, leading zeros, '-0', underscores, ints over 16 digits, numpy missing, any exception) falls back to plain sorted(map(int, data.split())). Evidence: in the final check the output matched expected.txt byte for byte on the 1M file, and t.py (17 cases) and chk.py (16 cases) reported no mismatches against the reference sort \u2014 these cover empty and all-whitespace input, mixed separators including \\r\\n, \\t, \\x0b and \\x0c, ints up to 10^30, and the fallback forms above. The assay (sh assay.sh, 1/best-of-9 seconds, prints 0 on any wrong output) printed 3.6492 on that final run. The last variants round adopted this version at a median of 3.893 against 3.442 for the previous body (+13%); the baseline registered with the assay was 2.1113, though scores drift between sessions so that longer comparison is rough.",
 "calls": 15,
 "secs": 1086.0
}
